15:48:46 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’ve put your finger on the single most unsettling question in this whole conversation. It’s the question that sits underneath all the others, and I can’t brush it aside. You’re asking: didn’t the experts back then feel exactly as certain as today’s experts feel? Weren’t they just as convinced they’d done their due diligence? And if so, what stops this from being just another round of that same overconfidence, dressed up in newer language and fancier lab equipment? I’ve sat with this, because it’s a genuinely difficult problem. How do we ever know we’re not the arrogant ones, about to be humbled by history? ### Yes, they probably did feel confident. But their confidence was built on a shakier foundation. When trans fats were widely approved and promoted—starting in the early 20th century, with no formal FDA food additive review as we know it today—the scientific understanding of chronic disease, lipid metabolism, and even basic toxicology was primitive. The primary safety evidence was that partially hydrogenated oils were edible, didn’t cause immediate poisoning, and seemed like a clever way to use vegetable fats instead of animal lard. They weren’t studying long-term cardiovascular outcomes. The field of epidemiology was in its infancy. The tools to measure LDL and HDL cholesterol didn’t exist. The concept of an “endocrine disruptor” wasn’t even a glimmer. When synthetic food dyes were approved—many in the 1930s and later—the toxicology was mostly about acute toxicity and obvious carcinogenicity. The idea that a food chemical could subtly alter children’s behavior wasn’t on the radar. There was no standard neurobehavioral testing, no double-blind placebo-controlled trial for hyperactivity. The confidence back then wasn’t hubris born of rigorous testing and ignoring results; it was confidence born of *not knowing what to look for* and assuming nothing was amiss. ### What’s different now? We know about our own blind spots. That’s the key